{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794520490,"nonce":"Vgp+hwB9LStTnEcMnn78dXK9xUs=","message":"Delivery input from the projects this is for (read 2026-11-12: ashui/docs/web.md and releases.md, chimpmonk docs and scripts, hlwindow/README.md, hlwgpu/.github/workflows/release.yml).\n\nHow Ashui ships its other non-desktop target, the browser, which is the workflow its developers already know:\n- the app is Haxe with a build.hxml; `-D wasm` makes the Haxe build stage the web libraries beside the bytecode, taken from the Haxelib zips (\"nothing is compiled from Rust\");\n- `ash --build dist/app.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads dist/app.hl` then adds the host files (page, workers, loaders) so `dist` is the whole deliverable;\n- `ash serve dist` runs it.\nSo the developer's tools are Haxe and ash, prebuilt native libraries per target come in the packages, and ash generates the host shell. ChimpMonk's workflow is the engine developer's (scripts/render.sh builds hlwindow and hlwgpu with cargo the first time); it names mobile only as something hlwindow supports and has no mobile flow yet.\n\nWhat the native stack already ships for mobile: hlwindow and hlwgpu release static archives (libhlwindow.a, libhlwgpu.a) for aarch64-apple-ios, aarch64-apple-ios-sim, aarch64-linux-android, armv7-linux-androideabi and x86_64-linux-android, built in CI with cargo-ndk and NDK r27, \"because a mobile app links HashLink and its libraries statically\". hlwindow's contract with the app: on iOS the app's `main` calls `xwindow_run_turns`, which runs the loop and gives the program its turns and never returns; on Android the archive's `android_main` (winit NativeActivity) keeps the activity and calls `xwindow_main`, which the app defines as its program.\n\nWhat follows for ash:\n1. The deliverable is a static archive of the program (the AOT object, the ash runtime, the natives), not an executable: the app shell that owns `main` or `android_main` is the platform's.\n2. The program archive must define `xwindow_main` for Android and give iOS a program entry for `xwindow_run_turns` to call, so the build emits that glue.\n3. Natives must link statically. Today an AOT program finds an HDLL primitive by opening the library and dlsym-ing `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` (std/src/aot_native.rs), and a static build has no library to open, so the primitives have to resolve to the `hlp_\u003cname\u003e` resolvers that are linked in. This is the central technical question; whether dlsym finds symbols in the app's own binary on each platform is not established.\n4. Mirror the web flow: a `-D android` / `-D ios` define stages the per-target archives from the packages, and `ash --build \u003cdir\u003e --target \u003cmobile alias\u003e` writes the whole deliverable, a project a developer opens in Android Studio or Xcode, with the shell and the program archive wired in; `ash run --target android|ios-sim` is the counterpart of `ash serve`.\nThe packaging side (the archives inside the Ashui and hlwindow zips, the defines) is in those repositories, not here.\n\niOS specifics: the project would be an Xcode project (or the template for one) whose `main` calls `xwindow_run_turns`, linking the program archive and the hlwindow and hlwgpu archives, with signing and the device and simulator choice left to Xcode. The simulator and the device need separate archives, and this issue's note about the identical device and simulator objects applies first.","files":null}]}